WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.910
Narrator: In this shot from
"Central Intelligence,"

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.350
a man on a motorcycle does a backflip

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.090
and then knocks The Rock's character over.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.300
But look behind the scenes

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.780
and you'll see nobody
was injured that day,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.160
because this shot was
actually two separate takes.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.200
This is a stunt trick
known as a camera lock-off.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.680
And it's just one of many
tried-and-true techniques

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.220
Hollywood uses in films and TV shows,

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.087
making even the most
unimaginable stunts come to life.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.260
In this scene in "Baby Driver,"

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.243
Ansel Elgort's character
pulls into a parking spot

00:00:36.243 --> 00:00:38.040
and then runs out moments later.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.020
In reality, that's a stunt
driver driving into the spot.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.440
The driver's side gets
covered by this pillar,

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.140
which is also hiding Elgort.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.040
So as Elgort runs out, it looks like

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.820
he's the one getting out of
the car in the first place.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.378
This is a trick known as a Texas switch,

00:00:52.378 --> 00:00:55.248
an in-camera effect in which
the actor and their double

00:00:55.248 --> 00:00:58.700
will actually switch places
midway through the action.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.810
The move involves close collaboration

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.830
between the stunt crew,
camera crew, and director.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.510
Whether it's a whip
off of the actual actor

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.960
to something to come right back to them,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.730
you can't just go focused on that person

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.290
and then put another person in.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.430
Narrator: And the set needs
a natural hiding place

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:14.680
for the switch to occur,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.260
like this rock that Sean
Connery is hiding behind

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.100
in "From Russia With Love."

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.200
The Texas switch isn't just for action.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.040
It's even become a comedic staple,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.240
in everything from "The Naked Gun"...

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.830
to "Scott Pilgrim vs. the World."

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.500
Knives Chau: Is Scott here?

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.508
Oh, you know what?

00:01:33.508 --> 00:01:35.250
[glass shattering]
He just left.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.450
Narrator: In this scene
in "Elf," for example,

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:38.920
Buddy needs to put a star

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.180
on top of a very tall Christmas tree.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.420
First, Will Ferrell runs off camera.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.830
Then the double runs out

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.263
and launches himself on top of the tree.

00:01:48.263 --> 00:01:50.380
[tree crashing]

00:01:53.647 --> 00:01:56.710
"Maleficent: Mistress of
Evil" contained many moments

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.370
where Angelina Jolie had to
perform dialogue in midair.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.240
So how exactly did they make
it look like she was flying

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.000
while allowing for her to comfortably

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.760
and safely act in the scene?

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.330
They used this device,
called a tuning fork,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.110
which consists of two
prongs that lock into

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.240
both sides of the performer's body.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.410
The rig is controlled by a steering wheel

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.800
that is driven by stuntpeople
in blue or green suits

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.840
who are erased in postproduction.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.770
Tuning forks were especially useful

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.120
for the shots where
Maleficent hovers in the air,

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.520
and the steering wheel makes
360-degree turns possible.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.730
This was key for scenes
where the Fey twist

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.760
and turn in the air in "Maleficent,"

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.080
or when Mulan performs
full spins in the air

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.710
in the live-action remake.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.390
You can also see it in
action in "Captain Marvel"

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:43.760
when Carol Danvers realizes
her full potential.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.660
But the fork isn't a complete
replacement for wires,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.660
which are still crucial for flight.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.218
According to stunt
coordinator Monique Ganderton,

00:02:51.218 --> 00:02:53.640
wirework helped give the
character more mobility

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.747
in "Captain Marvel" and "Endgame."

00:02:55.747 --> 00:02:58.120
She had two wires on her hips only,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.420
'cause I like them to have
freedom in their upper body.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.570
And then a guy in a blue suit behind

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.280
just kind of puppeteer the leg up,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.760
so her body could go side to side

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.703
and then come back up
into an upright position.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.420
Narrator: In "Men in
Black: International,"

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.610
a menacing alien tosses
Agent H down a staircase.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.990
To achieve this violent-looking motion,

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:24.060
filmmakers use a trick
called a ratchet pull.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.130
During a ratchet pull, a performer is tied

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.770
to cables powered by an
air-pressure cylinder,

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.210
which pulls them either
backwards, forwards, or upwards.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.910
The more pressure you use,
the farther they'll go.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.760
In "Twilight: Eclipse," for example,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:39.640
Monique Ganderton was
ratcheted 20 feet forward

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.230
after a CG wolf attacked her character.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.710
Sometimes a simplified
version of a ratchet pull

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.570
called a dead man will do.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:47.878
Instead of tying a performer

00:03:47.878 --> 00:03:50.085
to a complex rigging system off camera,

00:03:50.085 --> 00:03:51.290
the performer is tied to

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.640
a solid, stationary object in-frame.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.070
Monique: What happens is,
when you fully commit,

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.886
you run, and you jump,

00:03:57.886 --> 00:04:01.111
and you hit the end of that line,

00:04:01.111 --> 00:04:03.750
and it just whips you
back onto the ground.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:04.680
Narrator: You can see it performed

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.140
during the battle of
Wakanda in "Infinity War."

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.020
Monique: It gives for this violent,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:11.510
this really simple but violent action.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.040
Narrator: The dead man can also be used

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.620
to pull somebody off a horse,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:17.630
like you see here in this cut
sequence from "Chaos Walking."

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.050
And when Thor gets hit
by Thanos in "Endgame,"

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.800
both a traditional ratchet pull

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.680
and a dead man were actually needed

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.400
so Chris Hemsworth's double,
Bobby Holland Hanton,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.100
could be pulled forward and
then thrown to the ground.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.570
Getting a stunt performer
to go through glass,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.350
like in this scene from "The Hunt,"

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.950
requires a lot more force than
just a person's body weight.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.500
When a character needs to
jump or fall out a window,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.500
productions use tempered glass,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.630
which shatters into tiny, harmless bits

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.110
instead of sharp shards.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.270
But the glass itself is so strong

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.500
actors would simply bounce off it,

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.083
as you can see in this gag.

00:04:57.190 --> 00:04:59.380
So in these moments,
the special effects team

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:02.410
rigs the glass with
explosives timed to go off

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:05.140
the exact moment the
performer falls through.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.160
Daniel: By cracking it, you
weaken the whole system.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.450
Narrator: In "The Hunt," the doubles

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.180
were attached to a wire
that pulled them backwards

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:13.120
just as the explosives go off.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.783
Exploding glass also helped
hoist stuntman Daniel Hargrave

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:18.510
out a window in "The Accountant."

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.580
If you look on the ground,
you can see how tiny

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.070
the broken glass pieces actually are.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.770
Stuntpeople are finding efficient ways

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.400
to update this trick too.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.940
In "John Wick 2," the character

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.910
is thrown through a glass sculpture.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.710
Daniel: Basically the
performers had to hit the glass

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.410
and let the computerized
system do its thing.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.820
They put four charges that were
pressurized into the glass,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.050
so it was on a pressure system.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.750
As soon as that glass
pushed against the charger,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.408
it had, like, maybe a eighth
of an inch of pressure on it,

00:05:45.408 --> 00:05:48.179
so as soon as you'd push
through, it would blow.

00:05:48.179 --> 00:05:50.321
[glass shattering]

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.200
Narrator: This risky stunk
from "Central Intelligence,"

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.220
in which The Rock's Bob
Stone is hit in the head

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.430
by a motorcycle, was made possible

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:03.120
by clever camerawork and visual effects.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.340
In a trick known as a camera lock-off,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.670
two actions are shot
separately from a camera

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:10.890
that is locked into the
exact same place both times.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.410
Then those two shots are
combined using visual effects.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.880
First, filmmakers shot a
motorcyclist doing a backflip.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.870
Then, in another shot from the same angle,

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.430
stunt double Tanoai Reed was pulled back

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.690
on a ratchet to imitate impact.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.620
The wires were erased and the
two shots layered together

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.240
into a seamless whole.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.275
For moments like this,

00:06:30.275 --> 00:06:32.780
stuntpeople can actually get hit by cars.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:36.060
But lock-offs can lessen
the pain of vehicular hits.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.810
Take this moment in "Atomic Blonde,"

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:40.230
where Gascoigne, played by Sam Hargrave,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.240
is hit by a car and then smashed
into a van in front of him.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.410
Daniel: Sam was on a ratchet,

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:50.040
so he ran to his mark, and we
ratcheted him into the van.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.130
So no car hit him.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.378
And then I was driving
the car, and we just,

00:06:53.378 --> 00:06:56.040
same thing, lock-off, I
slid in as if I hit him,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.540
and then they merged together.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.150
And then he just turns, and
you see a wide shot of him

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.957
getting hit and flipped into this car.

00:07:02.957 --> 00:07:04.465
[grunts]

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.880
Narrator: In "Underworld,"
the werewolf Lucian

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.200
has to run at superhuman speed.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.240
And this isn't the work
of CGI or camera tricks.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.700
It's actually a practical
effect called a magic carpet.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:20.330
For this stunt, the crew will attach

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:22.320
a large tarp to a vehicle.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.750
The performer will start
running on the smooth surface

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.580
as the car drives, allowing
them to run much faster

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:28.817
than a normal human would.

00:07:28.817 --> 00:07:31.100
"Underworld" stunt coordinator Brad Martin

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.310
had actor Michael Sheen
and his stunt double

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:36.120
run on an 80-foot tarp attached to a car,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.410
creating the appearance of
running at 35 miles per hour.

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:40.920
It was so successful that Martin

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.220
used it again in "Underworld: Awakening."

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:45.370
It can make just about
any type of character

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:47.560
look like they have superhuman speed.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.730
Without a magic carpet,

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:51.000
the actors in 2001's "Planet of the Apes"

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.460
would never look as fast
as apes on all fours.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.850
And it's also why Black
Panther looks so fast

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.910
when he runs and jumps
onto the back of a car

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.730
in "Captain America: Civil War."

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.430
However, this really only works

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:03.750
on flat surfaces like asphalt.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.973
For off-road terrain, stunt crews may use

00:08:05.973 --> 00:08:08.570
a weighted wire system
attached to a winch,

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:10.103
basically a giant spool,

00:08:10.103 --> 00:08:12.753
to physically lift and propel them.

00:08:12.753 --> 00:08:14.810
Look at the start of the battle of Wakanda

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:16.380
in "Avengers: Infinity War"

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.220
when Captain America sprints
past his fellow Avengers,

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:21.280
who are also running at full speed.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:24.290
Stunt double Daniel Hargrave
was clipped into a harness

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.310
and attached to wires above his head.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.230
The winch was then weighted
to counteract his body weight,

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.050
helping him move up and forward.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:34.840
With the extra help, he could
run up to 24 miles per hour.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.320
Daniel: I was about 50% on my feet,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.480
so I still had to run and move
my feet as fast as possible,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.970
but I wasn't actually doing
all the propelling myself.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.130
Narrator: And it's helpful
with Marvel villains as well.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.440
In "The Incredible Hulk,"
a similar weighted system

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.550
helped Emil outrun several soldiers

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.130
after taking the superserum,

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.040
launching actor Tim Roth to speeds

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.763
ranging from 30 to 40 miles per hour.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.120
Some of Hollywood's most daring car stunts

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.890
need trained stunt drivers
to pull them off safely.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.810
But what if you still
want to make it appear

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.000
as if the actor is the one driving?

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.370
A much more dynamic way to capture chases

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.170
is by using a Biscuit.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.740
It's a vehicle that you
can put other vehicles on.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.150
So while the actors appear
to be driving on camera,

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.470
the real work is being
done by a stunt driver

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.960
sitting in a driver's
pod just out of frame.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.660
But because the stunt driver
isn't seen in a Biscuit rig,

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.890
the camera can capture
way more dynamic angles.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.250
These rigs allowed the
audience to see Christian Bale

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.400
up close in "Ford v Ferrari,"

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.870
and some seemingly impossible
car moves in "Baby Driver."

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.670
In the movie "Drive," they
actually shot this part twice,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.590
where Ryan Gosling's character
gets hit by another car.

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.210
First they shot the exteriors

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.250
with two actual stunt drivers,

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.120
then they shot Gosling on the Biscuit,

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.063
able to mimic every move to
get the inside of the car.

00:09:50.061 --> 00:09:52.780
Director: And ready, and action.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.740
Narrator: CGI can be used
to create big, safe fires.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.040
But fire is still one
of the hardest elements

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.740
to create with visual effects.

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:02.900
It's easier to set a
real stuntperson ablaze.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.370
So, how do they do it
without causing any harm?

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.540
Stunt performers often wear three layers

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.540
of fireproof underwear, which is soaked

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.110
in a flame-retardant
material called Zel Jel.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.150
They then add on three
more layers: a rain suit,

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.430
a fire suit, and a thin cotton suit.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.600
Even more Zel Jel will
go on any exposed skin.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.900
According to "Game of
Thrones" stunt coordinator

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:26.260
Rowley Irlam, timing
is key for fire stunts.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.950
A stunt performer can't be
fully engulfed in flames

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:31.182
for more than 15 seconds.

00:10:31.182 --> 00:10:32.232
Crew: Fire!

00:10:32.232 --> 00:10:35.790
One, two, three, four,

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:39.417
five, six, seven, eight.

00:10:39.417 --> 00:10:41.242
Out, out, out!

00:10:41.242 --> 00:10:42.480
Narrator: And you often have to account

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:44.340
for multiple stunt actors.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:47.450
Over 73 performers were set on fire

00:10:47.450 --> 00:10:50.080
for the loot train attack
in "Game of Thrones."

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.310
Full fire burns also come
with acting challenges.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.260
Performers need to hold their breath

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.460
while engulfed in flames

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.490
in order to not breathe
in any of the fire.

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.850
What stunt tricks do you
want to hear more about?

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.460
Let us know in the comments.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.130
Monique: Daniel and I did a winch drop

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.190
that was almost 600 feet
together on "Hunger Games."

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.030
It wasn't a hard gag for us,

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:12.490
but it's like, if something
goes wrong, you die,

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:15.250
because it's, like, 600 feet.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:16.190
But we had a great time.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.117
We just, for three days,
we just went up, and down,

00:11:19.117 --> 00:11:21.113
and up, and down. [laughing]

